---
import '../styles/prose.css';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SpecList from '../components/SpecList.astro';
import { site, mailto } from '../data/site';

const shortVersion = [
  { key: 'Started', val: 'Back of house — production, prep, food cost, labour, training' },
  { key: 'Now', val: "Operations at B’s Bagels, Indiana, and building software around it" },
  { key: 'Built', val: 'An internal operations portal. An AI invoice system, in progress.' },
  { key: 'Want', val: 'AI implementation, solutions, product operations, ops technology' },
  { key: 'Not', val: 'A career software engineer — and not claiming to be one' },
];

const working = [
  {
    title: 'I start on the floor',
    body: 'Before anything gets designed, I want to watch the work happen. Requirements gathered in a meeting describe the process people believe they follow. The one they actually follow is different, and the difference is the whole job.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Process before software',
    body: 'A surprising number of things people want built are sequencing problems wearing a software costume. Reordering the work is faster, cheaper and more likely to stick. I check for that first, even when building would be more fun.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Small, then real',
    body: 'I would rather have one procedure that people genuinely use than a complete system nobody opens. Real usage is the only signal that means anything, and you get it earliest by shipping something narrow.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Design for the bad day',
    body: 'Not the demo. The understaffed Saturday, the new hire on their third shift, the manager who is off. If it only works when things are calm, it does not work.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Say what is actually true',
    body: 'About status, about what is finished, about what the software can and cannot do. Overstating something buys a week and costs the relationship. This site is labelled that way on purpose.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Background"
  description="How Cameron Macek got from back-of-house restaurant operations to building internal tools and AI workflows — and what he's looking for next."
>
  <article class="bg">
    <header class="shell shell--narrow bg__header">
      <p class="label">Background</p>
      <h1 class="bg__title">I didn&rsquo;t set out to build software.</h1>
      <p class="bg__deck">
        I set out to stop losing the same twenty minutes every morning to a question somebody had
        already answered.
      </p>
      <SpecList items={shortVersion} class="bg__spec" />
    </header>

    <div class="shell shell--narrow rich bg__body">
      <section>
        <h2><span class="n">01 / Operations</span>An unglamorous education, and a fast one.</h2>
        <p>
          Most of what I know I learned in the back of house at B&rsquo;s Bagels in Indiana.
          Production and prep. Food cost and recipes. Labour. Training new people. Inventory.
          Cleaning standards. The ordinary work of keeping a kitchen consistent when almost none of
          the inputs are &mdash; not the people, not the volume, not the product, not the weather.
        </p>
        <p>
          It teaches you quickly which problems are real, because the real ones charge you the same
          amount every single week. You stop being interested in problems that are merely annoying
          and start being interested in the ones with a number attached.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2><span class="n">02 / The turn</span>It was never a knowledge problem.</h2>
        <p>
          The thing I kept running into wasn&rsquo;t that nobody knew how to do the work. People
          knew. It was a <strong>distribution</strong> problem: the correct answer existed, but not
          in the place or the moment somebody needed it.
        </p>
        <p>
          So I wrote things down. Then I started reorganising the processes that were producing the
          confusion in the first place. And when good documentation kept failing at the last mile
          &mdash; the actual moment of need, hands full, timer running, three people waiting &mdash;
          I started building.
        </p>
        <p class="pull">
          Software was my third answer, not my first. I think that&rsquo;s the right order, and
          it&rsquo;s most of what I&rsquo;d bring to a company building tools for operators.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2><span class="n">03 / AI</span>Useful, and not to be trusted without a plan.</h2>
        <p>
          I use AI every day and I&rsquo;m not precious about it. It reads invoices, drafts
          procedures, structures a decade of scattered notes, and absorbs the volume of text work
          that used to make thorough documentation uneconomical for a small business. That&rsquo;s a
          genuine change, and it&rsquo;s the reason several things I&rsquo;m building are now worth
          building.
        </p>
        <p>
          I&rsquo;m at least as interested in the failure modes. Most of my design time on anything
          AI-assisted goes into what happens <em>after</em> the model answers: how confident was it,
          what gets checked, who checks it, how the correction gets remembered, and what it costs
          when something wrong slips through anyway.
        </p>
        <p>
          That isn&rsquo;t scepticism. It&rsquo;s the difference between a demo and something an
          operator will still be using in March, when their margin depends on the number being
          right.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2><span class="n">04 / How I work</span>Five habits, honestly held.</h2>
        <ul class="rows">
          {
            working.map((item) => (
              <li>
                <b>{item.title}</b>
                {item.body}
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2><span class="n">05 / What&rsquo;s next</span>Where I&rsquo;d be genuinely useful.</h2>
        <p>
          Roles where understanding the customer&rsquo;s actual business is the hard part. AI
          implementation and AI operations. Solutions and implementation work. Customer
          implementation and technical customer success. Product operations. Operations technology
          and workflow automation.
        </p>
        <p>
          Restaurant and hospitality software is the obvious fit, because I already speak the
          language and I know what an operator will and won&rsquo;t do during service. But the
          pattern isn&rsquo;t restaurant-specific. Any frontline business &mdash; clinics, shops,
          field service, logistics &mdash; has the same structure: a real process that only partly
          matches the documented one, knowledge concentrated in a few people, and software that has
          to survive contact with a bad day.
        </p>
        <p>
          What I bring is the translation layer. I can sit with an operator and understand their
          week, and I can sit with the product and understand what it can actually do. Companies
          building for operators usually have plenty of the second and not much of the first.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="bg__cta-section">
        <h2><span class="n">06 / Contact</span>Easiest way to reach me.</h2>
        <p>
          Email, and I&rsquo;ll reply. Whether it&rsquo;s a role, a question about something on this
          site, or a restaurant that needs its operation written down before the person who knows it
          leaves.
        </p>
        <div class="bg__actions">
          <a class="btn btn--primary" href={mailto('Hello Cameron')}>
            {site.email} <span class="btn__arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
          </a>
          <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/#work">See the work</a>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .bg__header {
    padding-top: clamp(1.5rem, 0.5rem + 4vw, 3.5rem);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .bg__title {
    margin-top: var(--space-m);
    font-size: var(--step-4);
    letter-spacing: -0.033em;
    max-width: 15ch;
  }

  .bg__deck {
    margin-top: var(--space-s);
    font-family: var(--font-prose);
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    line-height: 1.35;
    letter-spacing: -0.012em;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    max-width: 40ch;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .bg__spec {
    margin-top: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .bg__body {
    padding-bottom: var(--space-2xl);
  }

  .bg__actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-xs);
    margin-top: var(--space-m);
  }

  .bg__cta-section :global(.btn--primary) {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
</style>
